#!/usr/bin/python3
"""OmacVM.app: Omarchy on every Mac display.

In full screen, OmacVM.app opens a window on every Mac display and gives the
VM one output per display (Virtual-1 is the main window, Virtual-2 ... the
others). This agent is the VM's side of that, over the virtio port
org.omacvm.display, one JSON object per line:

  from the Mac  {"layout": [{"output": "Virtual-2", "x": 1728, "y": 0,
                 "width": 1920, "height": 1080, "scale": 1}, ...],
                 "external": true, "fullscreen": true, "builtin": "Virtual-2"}
                where each output sits in the Mac's arrangement (points);
                "builtin" names the output on the Mac's own display (the
                notch), when one is;
  to the Mac    {"hello": 1, "external": true, "pause": 1}  when it starts
                ("pause": it can turn displays off for "unseen"),
                {"external": false}             when the switch changes,
                {"monitors": [{"name": "Virtual-1", "x": 0, "y": 0,
                 "width": 1728, "height": 1085}, ...]}
                where Hyprland put its outputs (logical pixels), so the
                Mac points the pointer at the right one,
                {"scale": 1.6}                  the scale the main output
                wants (Omarchy's omarchy_monitor_scale, or the one just
                picked): in "Full screen including notch" the Mac gives it a
                size that scale fits, a few pixels more than the panel, shown
                scaled onto it (#355); the Mac keeps it for the next start.
  from the Mac  {"unseen": ["Virtual-1"]}  the outputs whose Mac window
                nobody can see now (OmacVM.app's "Pause drawing while
                hidden"): the agent turns their display off (DPMS), so
                Hyprland and the apps stop drawing, as macOS apps do for a
                window nobody sees; {"unseen": []} turns them on again. Only
                displays the agent turned off itself are turned on, and none
                is turned off while a screen share or recording of it runs
                (Hyprland's screencastv2 events; any window share keeps all
                of them on). The list of them is in
                $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/omacvm/paused-displays, so a restarted agent
                turns them on first (the Mac tells it again at its hello).
                The Mac says "unseen" again every 5 s while it holds (a
                line can get lost): with no word for 15 s the agent turns its
                displays on. After a start or a lost event socket it waits
                3 s for Hyprland's share events before it turns one off.
                While a display is off this way, Hyprland's own wake-ups
                (misc:mouse_move_enables_dpms, misc:key_press_enables_dpms,
                on in Omarchy) are off too: they turn EVERY display on at
                any move or key, also one on another display, and the Mac's
                next "unseen" turned it off again. Only options that were on
                are turned off; they are back once nothing is paused, when
                another display goes off by itself (Omarchy's idle wakes it
                the usual way), and at the agent's next start
                ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/omacvm/paused-wake). A config reload puts
                Omarchy's values back: they are turned off again.

Hyprland's event socket has no event when an output only moves (display-sync
and Omanotch move them with hyprctl eval, a config reload puts them back to
"auto" for a moment). A stale report leaves the pointer in part of the screen
(only the left half, say), so the agent learns of moves three ways: the Lua
hook monitor.layout_changed (omacvm_app.lua) runs `omacvm-displays poke`; for
15 s after any change it compares the layout twice a second; and it compares
it every 10 s anyway.

The layout goes to $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/omacvm/displays.json, where
omacvm-display-sync takes the positions from, and the built-in display's
output to $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/omacvm/builtin (missing: none of them), which
Omanotch follows.

The switch "Use external displays" is ~/.config/omacvm/displays.conf
(external=on or off; on when missing):

  omacvm-displays                   run the agent (the user service)
  omacvm-displays external on|off|toggle
  omacvm-displays state             "on" or "off" (nothing without the port)
  omacvm-displays status            the switch and the outputs, as JSON
  omacvm-displays desktop           displays that show no desktop, as JSON
  omacvm-displays poke              tell the agent the layout moved (Hyprland's hook)
  omacvm-displays pause             whether it can pause drawing, the displays it turned off and
                                    Hyprland's wake options it holds off meanwhile, as JSON
  omacvm-displays fullpanel         the output in "Full screen including notch" (nothing otherwise)
  omacvm-displays scale SCALE       Omarchy's scale for the focused output (the display
                                    panel's buttons): in "Full screen including notch" the Mac
                                    first gives a size that fits it, then Omarchy sets it

A display can come up without wallpaper or bar, only Hyprland's dark grey:
when the shell lost its GPU context (QEMU refused a large texture's memory,
fixed in OmacVM.app 2.8.0; any other GPU fault does the same) or when its
wallpaper stayed at an output's old place. The agent looks at what each
display really shows (a small screenshot) after the shell starts and after
the layout changes. A display that shows only Hyprland's background with no
window on it (in HDR the screenshot has nothing there at all: alpha 0, see
png_shown), twice 4 s apart, gets the shell restarted (what fixed it by
hand; Omanotch's bar parks again from its park file): not while the screen
is locked, at most every 2 minutes and 3 times per session, and not again
when a restart changed nothing. repair-shell=off in displays.conf turns the
restart off; the check and its log line stay.

The switch also sits in Omarchy's display panel (the bar widget
omacvm.monitor, built from Omarchy's own by monitor-widget/build.py; rebuilt
here when Omarchy's panel changes with an update).
"""

